<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludovico Antonio Muratori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1672">1672</a>–<a href="/years/1750">1750</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludovico Antonio Muratori (1672–1750) was an Italian priest and historian, librarian of the Este dukes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> from 1700. His Rerum Italicarum Scriptores collected the sources of medieval Italian history and became the basis of its critical study. He also discovered the Muratorian fragment, the oldest known list of books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludovico-antonio-muratori.jpg" alt="Ludovico Antonio Muratori" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_antonio_cybei,_ritratto_di_ludovico_antonio_muratori,_1774_(modena,_biblioteca_estense_universitaria).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lodovico Antonio Muratori was born on 21 October 1672 at Vignola, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, to a poor family. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> gave him his first instruction, and he went on to study law, philosophy and theology at the University of Modena. He was ordained a priest in 1694. The following year he was called to the college of Dottori at the Ambrosian Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, where he began at once to collect unedited ancient writings. His first publication was the Anecdota Latina ex Ambrosianæ Bibliothecæ codicibus, in two volumes (Milan, 1697–98), followed by two more at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> in 1713.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1700 Duke Rinaldo I made him archivist and librarian of the ducal library at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, a post he held until his death. In 1716 he also became provost of Santa Maria della Pomposa, which he served until 1733. He kept publishing unedited texts and also wrote on literature, among them Della perfetta poesia italiana (Modena, 1706). In 1703, under the pseudonym Lamindo Pritanio, he published a plan for a learned society of Italian literature.</p><p>In 1708 the Holy See and the Este dukes quarrelled over Comacchio, a dispute that touched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. Muratori backed his sovereign with historical research, which he later used in the Antichità Estensi ed Italiane (1717 and 1740). The Rerum Italicarum Scriptores, covering the years 500 to 1500, appeared in twenty-eight folio volumes at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> between 1723 and 1751, with the help of the Società Palatina, Filippo Argelati and Count Carlo Archinto. Its companion, the Antiquitates italicæ medii ævi (six volumes, 1738 to 1742), gathered seventy-five essays, and its third volume contains the Muratorian Canon. He also wrote a chronological Annali d’Italia in twelve volumes (1744 to 1749).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His religious writings drew criticism. De ingeniorum moderatione in religionis negotio (1714) asked how far freedom of thought might go in religious matters, and Della regolata divozione de’ Cristiani (1747) was attacked, which he answered in De superstitione vitanda (1742). Even so, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv">Benedict XIV</a> and Cardinal Ganganelli, later Clement XIV, wrote to him with esteem. In 1717 he was elected a Fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> on the recommendation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> on 23 January 1750 and was buried in Santa Maria della Pomposa. Historians count him among the fathers of modern historiography, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> called him “my guide and master in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>”.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludovico Antonio Muratori” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Este rebuilt Modena as a capital, with a ducal palace and the Biblioteca Estense, where the scholar <b>Ludovico Antonio Muratori</b> worked as librarian.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vignola, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.48,11&amp;year=1722" aria-label="Vignola on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4813 591 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ludovico Antonio Muratori"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5263 861h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5263 861h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.48,11&amp;year=1722">What was here in 1722? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
